nd given what we know about trans  fats and their health risks this is not  ideal  no  however the amount of trans fats in  canola oil specifically is one highly  variable and two comparable to other  oils for example one study comparing the  trans fat content of multiple oils found  that on average the commercial canola  oil can contain anywhere from 1.9  to 3.6 trans fats whereas walnut oil  contains anywhere from two to three  point nine percent but for some reason  we don't see walnut oil getting the same  negative pr as canola oil regardless  like all things related to nutrition  context is important here and the amount  of trans fat that you would reasonably  consume from canola oil or any other oil  is unlikely to cause serious harm in the  context of a balanced diet so to put it  in perspective the who recommends  limiting trans fat intake to less than  2.2 grams a day for the average 2000  calorie diet with that in mind one  tablespoon of canola oil provides  roughly .2 grams of trans fat and you  can divide that by four if you're using  that oil to cook a meal for a family of  four and if you are still concerned  about the impact of the refining process  on nutritional content and fat  composition the good news is that you  can absolutely purchase canola oil that  is minimally processed or unrefined by  looking for the label cold pressed or  expeller pressed these oils are  definitely less processed but the  trade-off is that they're less stable  have a shorter shelf life and are more  expensive so that's why we really don't  see them used in large scale food  processing very much so what is my  overall take on canola oil well  ultimately in my eyes  canola actually has a generally very  healthy fatty acid composition it may be  20 omega-6 but it's actually 60 percent  monounsaturated fats and 10  omega-3 fats meaning it has the ideal  two-to-one ratio of omega-6 to omega-3s  that we know is associated with better  health i think wellness culture has used  it as a scapegoat unnecessarily simply  because it's consumed in excess via  ultra processed foods and it's the  excess consumption of ultra processed  foods that's the much bigger fish to fry  so i say if you're getting the majority  of your fats from whole food sources in  your day and then cooking your  vegetables or your fish in regular  canola oil or enjoying the occasional  refined convenience food made with  canola oil folks it's all gonna net out  as a generally very healthy diet but if  we're eating the majority of our  calories in ultra-processed convenience  food that happens to be prepared with  canola oil well i would say that that is  the pattern that we would probably want  to try to shift and if you're still  concerned because you're using your  cooking oil as your primary source of  fat then choosing a cold pressed oil may  be a better nutrition bang for your buck  so on that note i hope this information  helped to lift some food fear and make  you a more informed and critical  consumer and like i've said before the  focus should be on overall dietary  pattern not on specific nutrients or  ingredients in any given food so if you  like this video be sure to give it the  thumbs up leave me a comment below on  which food additives you want to see me  discuss next a big thank you again to  betterhelp for sponsoring this video  subscribe to this channel and i will see  you next time on abby's kitchen bye  [Music]
 

